Abstract

This dissertation develops the theoretical foundations of Profession 20: the Autonomous Supply Network Architect. We construct a rigorous mathematical model of a lunar autonomous supply network (ASN) as a tuple of sources, depots, transformation nodes, directed transport arcs with per‑resource capacity and latency, local production functions, and stochastic demand. Within this framework, three fundamental theorems are proved, each establishing a binding design constraint that elevates supply network architecture from a logistical optimization problem to a survival‑critical discipline.

Theorem 1 (Survivability Bound via Min‑Cut) establishes that the sustainable consumption rate of any critical resource at a colony node is bounded above by the minimum‑capacity cut separating it from all sources in a pure‑transport subnetwork, plus the node’s own ISRU production. The proof adapts the max‑flow min‑cut theorem under a zero‑net‑demand assumption on intermediate nodes and shows that no control policy can exceed this topological limit—formalizing ISRU as the only term that bypasses the cut.

Theorem 2 (Buffer Sizing Under Budgeted Uncertainty) transposes the robust inventory theory of Bertsimas and Thiele to the lunar environment. We show that under a budget‑of‑uncertainty demand model with proportional deviations, the required safety buffer scales linearly with the product of resupply latency and the nominal demand rate—quantifying the penalty of Earth‑dependence in a formal, non‑probabilistic way. The optimality of an $(s,S)$ policy under fixed ordering cost follows from prior robust‑dynamic‑programming results.

Theorem 3 (Latency–Autonomy Impossibility) is a domain‑specific CAP‑style result. We prove that no deterministic Earth‑resident centralized control policy can simultaneously achieve (a) decision latency below twice the one‑way Earth–Moon communication delay, (b) full‑network state consistency, and (c) robustness to a single link failure, for any ASN whose safety requires coordinating at least two surface nodes. The proof uses an indistinguishability argument mirroring the FLP impossibility, instantiated in interplanetary delay physics.

Together, the three theorems form a cohesive design discipline: the Architect raises survivability by either increasing the min‑cut (transport capacity) or increasing local production; buffer sizing must incorporate latency‑amplified demand volatility; and any control loop requiring sub‑round‑trip‑time response must be executed autonomously on the lunar surface. The dissertation concludes with a synthesis into a layered control architecture (local MPC, neighbor‑only consensus, Earth‑based digital‑twin planning) and identifies open problems including the distributed‑information gap between the min‑cut bound and local‑only coordination, endogenous pricing of ISRU, and extension to the three‑body Earth–Moon–Mars system.

