Chapter 1. Introduction: The Structural Necessity of Autonomy

1.1 Lunar Supply as a Topological Problem

Traditional supply chain management frames the problem as cost minimization subject to demand satisfaction. On the Moon, this framing is insufficient. An interruption of oxygen, water, or power does not incur a financial penalty; it terminates the colony. Therefore, the binding constraint is survivability—the ability to sustain nominal consumption under the worst‑case conjunction of demand spikes, transport failures, and communication latency.

Independent ISRU architecture studies project lunar‑derived propellant becoming cost‑competitive with Earth‑launched propellant within a decade [NASA, 2025]. However, cost‑competitiveness is not the critical threshold. NASA’s own ISRU program logic identifies Earth‑independence as a survival requirement, not an optimization [Science, 2024]. This shifts the theoretical problem from “optimize a supply chain” to “design a supply network that remains live under all physically possible shocks.”

1.2 From Supply Chain to Autonomous Supply Network

The terrestrial autonomous supply chain (ASC) literature, though nascent, converges on a diagnosis: human‑mediated, centrally‑scheduled chains are structurally brittle to global shocks (COVID‑19, geopolitical fracture). The remedy is to push decision‑making to the edge—predictive, self‑decision‑making nodes that act on local information [Xu et al., 2023, arXiv:2310.09435]. The lunar case takes this same argument to its limit. With a one‑way light‑time delay of ≈ 1.3 s and no possibility of sending a human repair crew, the network must self‑heal.

This dissertation formalizes that requirement. We construct a mathematical model that captures what an autonomous supply network is and what no control policy can circumvent. The three theorems that follow are not algorithmic improvements; they are impossibility results and necessary bounds that define the design space.

