Chapter 2. Formal Model of an Autonomous Supply Network (ASN)

We define the ASN with sufficient precision to support proofs in subsequent chapters. The model distinguishes between resources (oxygen, water, energy, spares, etc.) but, unless otherwise noted, we analyse one resource at a time; a complete multicommodity treatment is reserved for future work.

Definition 2.1 (Autonomous Supply Network, $\mathcal{N}$).
An autonomous supply network is a tuple

\mathcal{N} = (V, E, \mathcal{C}, \tau, \Phi, D)

where

· $V = V_S \cup V_D \cup V_T$ is a finite set of nodes, partitioned into:
   – $V_S$: source nodes (ISRU plants, Earth launch pads that inject mass into the network);
   – $V_D$: depot/buffer nodes (storage, aggregation points, habitats that consume resources);
   – $V_T$: transformation nodes (electrolysis stacks, regolith processors, 3D‑printers).
· $E \subseteq V \times V$ is a set of directed transport arcs (rover paths, hopper trajectories, tethers).
· $\mathcal{C} = \{C_k\}_{k \in \mathcal{R}}$ assigns a per‑resource capacity function $C_k : E \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ (mass per unit time for commodity $k$) to each arc. The physical arc has a total capacity that may be shared; $C_k(e)$ is the maximum rate at which resource $k$ can be conveyed.
· $\tau: E \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ assigns a transit plus communication latency. For arcs crossing the Earth–Moon gap, $\tau$ includes the one‑way light‑time and queuing delay.
· $\Phi = \{\phi_v\}_{v \in V_T}$ is a collection of local transformation functions; $\phi_v$ maps a vector of input resource flows into outputs, with the physical constraint that output rates cannot exceed stoichiometric maxima given inputs and available energy.
· $D: V_D \times \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ is a stochastic demand process, where for each depot $v$ and time $t$, $D(v,t)$ gives the instantaneous consumption rate of a composite resource bundle. For modeling flexibility we may, in different chapters, specialise this to a Poisson‑jump process or to a bounded‑uncertainty model with no distributional assumption.

Definition 2.2 (Feasible Flow and Stockout).
For a single resource $k$, a flow is a function $f: E \times \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ such that $0 \le f(e,t) \le C_k(e)$ for all $e,t$. For any node $v$, mass conservation must hold at every instant:

\frac{dB_v}{dt} = \sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} f(e,t) - \sum_{e \in \delta^+(v)} f(e,t) + P_v(t) - D(v,t),

where $B_v(t)$ is the buffer stock, $\delta^-(v)$ are incoming arcs, $\delta^+(v)$ outgoing arcs, and $P_v(t)$ is the local production rate (zero for $v \notin V_T$, and non‑negative for source nodes). Additionally, the buffer must satisfy the physical constraint $B_v(t) \ge 0$ for all $t$; any instant where demand would drive $B_v$ negative constitutes an unmet‑demand event (stockout) and is considered a failure.

Definition 2.3 (Autonomy Horizon).
For a resource $k$, the autonomy horizon $H_k(v)$ at node $v$ is the supremum of time durations $\theta$ such that, starting from current buffer levels, the colony can sustain nominal consumption of $k$ using only local buffer stock and local production, assuming zero further external resupply and no recovery of failed transport links beyond current redundancy.

Definition 2.4 (Resilience Trajectory).
The resilience of $\mathcal{N}$ following a shock at time $t_0$ is the function

\rho_{\mathcal{N}}(t) = \frac{\text{Performance}(\mathcal{N}, t \mid \text{shock at } t_0)}{\text{Performance}(\mathcal{N}, t \mid \text{no shock})},

where performance is a scalar measure (e.g., fraction of nominal demand met) and $\rho_{\mathcal{N}}(t) \to 1$ signals full recovery.

