Chapter 3. Theorem 1: Survivability Bound via Minimum Cut

3.1 Statement and Physical Interpretation

Theorem 3.1 (Survivability Bound for a Single Resource).
Let $\mathcal{N}$ be an ASN, and fix a resource $k$. Consider a target depot node $v \in V_D$ and a set $S \subseteq V_S$ of external source nodes that supply $k$. Assume that all nodes other than $S$ and $v$ are pure transit with respect to $k$: their own net demand for $k$ is zero, and any local production exactly offsets their own consumption. Let $C_k(e)$ be the capacity of arc $e$ dedicated to $k$, and let $\dot{P}_v(k)$ denote the maximum rate at which $v$ can produce $k$ from local ISRU transformation (possibly zero). Then any feasible flow policy that keeps $B_v(t) \ge 0$ for all $t$ can sustain a steady‑state consumption rate $r(v)$ satisfying

r(v) \le \operatorname{mincut}_k(\mathcal{N}, S, v) + \dot{P}_v(k),

where $\operatorname{mincut}_k(\mathcal{N}, S, v)$ is the minimum capacity of a directed cut separating $S$ from $v$ in the graph $(V, E)$ with arc capacities $C_k(e)$.

Interpretation. The term $\operatorname{mincut}_k$ is a purely topological bottleneck: no routing algorithm, AI planner, or dynamic re‑scheduling can push more mass through the transport network than the narrowest constriction along all paths from sources to $v$. The only way to increase the sustainable consumption rate above this bottleneck is to add production at $v$ itself—the ISRU term—which bypasses the transport graph entirely. The zero‑net‑demand assumption on intermediate nodes ensures they do not act as sinks that divert flow away from $v$; relaxing this assumption would lower the achievable bound further, making the inequality even tighter.

3.2 Proof

Proof. Consider a steady state in which buffer levels $B_v$ are constant on average over a long interval $[0,T]$, and $B_v(t) \ge 0$ holds everywhere. Integrating mass conservation at node $v$:

\frac{1}{T}\int_0^T \frac{dB_v}{dt} dt = \frac{B_v(T)-B_v(0)}{T} \to 0 \quad \text{as } T\to\infty .

Hence the time‑averaged net inflow equals the time‑averaged consumption:

\lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T}\int_0^T \Big( \sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} f(e,t) - \sum_{e \in \delta^+(v)} f(e,t) + P_v(t) \Big) dt = \bar{r}(v),

where $\bar{r}(v)$ is the average consumption rate.
Local production $P_v(t)$ is bounded above by $\dot{P}_v(k)$, so

\bar{r}(v) \le \lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T}\int_0^T \Big( \sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} f(e,t) \Big) dt + \dot{P}_v(k),

since outgoing flows only reduce net inflow.

Now consider the network with a supersource $s^*$ connected to all $s \in S$ by infinite‑capacity arcs. Because every intermediate node between $S$ and $v$ has zero net demand for $k$, flow is conserved at those nodes exactly as required by the classical max‑flow min‑cut theorem (the same holds for any node in $V_S\setminus S$ that might appear on paths; they are sources themselves and are fed into the supersource). The total flow entering $v$ from the network is the value of an $s^*$–$v$ flow. By the max‑flow min‑cut theorem (Ford & Fulkerson, 1956), the maximum possible time‑averaged flow from $s^*$ to $v$ equals the minimum capacity of an $s^*$–$v$ cut. Because the additional arcs from $s^*$ have infinite capacity, any finite cut must reside entirely in the original edge set $E$, and its capacity is exactly $\operatorname{mincut}_k(\mathcal{N}, S, v)$. Hence for any feasible flow,

\lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T}\int_0^T \sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} f(e,t) dt \le \operatorname{mincut}_k(\mathcal{N}, S, v).

Substituting, we obtain

\bar{r}(v) \le \operatorname{mincut}_k(\mathcal{N}, S, v) + \dot{P}_v(k).

A steady‑state sustainable rate $r(v)$ is defined as a consumption rate that can be maintained indefinitely with $B_v(t)\ge 0$; any such rate is a particular time‑average, and the inequality applies. $\blacksquare$

Remark. The bound is tight under the pure‑transit assumption: if the min‑cut is the bottleneck and local production is zero, a network that saturates the bottleneck exactly achieves $r(v)=\operatorname{mincut}_k$. The theorem justifies the central design heuristic: to raise survivability, increase either transport capacity (by adding arcs or widening bottlenecks) or in‑situ production capacity. No other lever exists.

