Chapter 4. Theorem 2: Robust Buffer Sizing Under Budgeted Uncertainty

4.1 The Inventory Problem on the Moon

Consider a single buffer node $v$ that replenishes a resource from a remote source (e.g., an ISRU plant at another crater) with a known resupply latency $\tau_{\text{resupply}}$ (the time from placing an order to receiving the shipment). Demand is uncertain but bounded. Following the robust optimization approach of Bertsimas and Thiele (2006), we do not assume a probability distribution; instead, we confine demand uncertainty to a budget‑of‑uncertainty set. This choice is deliberate: on the Moon, demand distributions are poorly characterized, whereas engineering bounds can be conservatively estimated.

4.2 Robust Inventory Model

Time is discrete, indexed by $t=1,2,\dots$ with a period length of $\Delta t$. Let $d_t$ be the demand in period $t$, modelled as

d_t = \bar{d} + \delta \cdot z_t, \qquad z_t \in [-1, 1],

where $\bar{d}$ is the nominal demand rate per period and $\delta$ is the maximum absolute deviation. The uncertainty budget $\Gamma$ limits the total scaled deviation over the resupply window of length $L = \lceil \tau_{\text{resupply}} / \Delta t \rceil$:

\sum_{t=1}^{L} |z_t| \le \Gamma, \qquad 0 \le \Gamma \le L.

This set captures the idea that not all periods can simultaneously be “worst‑case.”
Let $x_t$ be the inventory level at the start of period $t$. When inventory falls to or below a reorder point $s$, an order of size $S - x_t$ is placed, raising inventory to $S$ after the latency $L$ (zero lead‑time variability for clarity). Holding and shortage costs are linear with coefficients $h$ and $p$, respectively. A fixed cost $K$ is incurred per order. The objective is to minimise the worst‑case total cost over a finite horizon $T$, subject to the inventory dynamics and the uncertainty set.

Modeling Assumption (Proportional Deviations). We assume that the deviation bound scales with the nominal demand, i.e., $\delta = \kappa \bar{d}$ for some coefficient of variation $\kappa > 0$. This is the natural lunar analogue of terrestrial demand volatility scaling with mean consumption.

4.3 Optimal Policy Structure and Buffer Sizing

Theorem 4.1 (Robust $(s,S)$ Optimality).
Under the budget‑of‑uncertainty demand model with fixed ordering cost $K$, the optimal replenishment policy that minimises the worst‑case cost over a finite horizon is of the $(s,S)$ type: there exist thresholds $s_v$ and $S_v$ such that an order is placed if and only if $x_t \le s_v$, and the order‑up‑to level is $S_v$.

Justification. This is a known result in robust inventory theory. Bertsimas and Thiele (2006) prove that, without a fixed cost, a base‑stock policy is optimal under a polyhedral (budgeted) uncertainty set. The extension to a fixed cost preserves $K$‑convexity of the worst‑case cost‑to‑go function, and the classical proof of Scarf (1960) then establishes the optimality of an $(s,S)$ policy. The technical verification of $K$‑convexity for multi‑period robust dynamic programs with a time‑coupled budget constraint is carried out in See and Sim (2010) for closely related settings; the argument for the present model follows the same lines and is omitted for brevity. ∎

Lemma 4.2 (Buffer Sizing).
Under the proportional‑deviation assumption, the required safety buffer $S_v - s_v$ scales linearly with the nominal demand rate $\bar{d}$ and, when the uncertainty budget $\Gamma$ is chosen as a fixed fraction of the lead‑time length $L$, scales linearly with the resupply latency $\tau_{\text{resupply}}$.

Proof. After placing an order, the inventory position is raised to $S_v$. Over the next lead time $L$, the worst‑case cumulative demand is

D_{\max}(L) = \bar{d} L + \delta \cdot \Gamma = \bar{d} L + \kappa \bar{d} \Gamma.

To avoid a stockout with certainty under the worst‑case scenario, we require $S_v \ge D_{\max}(L)$. The reorder point $s_v$ is typically set to cover the nominal lead‑time demand plus a safety component; the buffer (order‑up‑to minus nominal demand over lead time) must then cover the excess worst‑case deviation $\kappa \bar{d} \Gamma$. Hence

S_v - s_v \ge \kappa \bar{d} \Gamma.

If the uncertainty budget is a fixed fraction $\theta$ of the lead‑time length, $\Gamma = \theta L$, then

S_v - s_v \ge \kappa \theta \bar{d} L = \kappa \theta \bar{d} \lceil \tau_{\text{resupply}} / \Delta t \rceil \approx \frac{\kappa \theta}{\Delta t} \bar{d} \, \tau_{\text{resupply}},

which is linear in $\bar{d}$ and in $\tau_{\text{resupply}}$. ∎

Design Consequence. The buffer size grows multiplicatively with communication latency. For Earth‑dependent resupply, $\tau_{\text{resupply}}$ includes weeks or months of Earth‑launch‑window delays, causing buffers to balloon. Lunar‑based ISRU shortens $\tau_{\text{resupply}}$ to hours (surface transport), dramatically reducing the buffer penalty. This formalises the inventory‑theoretic argument for lunar ISRU, independent of production cost.

