Chapter 5. Theorem 3: Latency–Autonomy Impossibility

5.1 Centralized Control Model

We consider a distributed system of $n$ nodes (sources, depots, transformation nodes) interconnected by communication links that may fail. The control goal is to enforce a set of safety constraints (e.g., no buffer stockout, no thermal runaway) that require coordinated actions among at least two distinct surface nodes. A centralized deterministic control policy $\mathcal{C}_{\text{Earth}}$ resides on Earth. It receives state messages from all nodes, computes a joint control command, and sends commands back. Because Earth is at a distance $\Delta$ (one‑way light‑time ≈ 1.3 s for the Moon), each round‑trip communication takes at least $2\Delta$ seconds, ignoring processing time.

We formally define the three desiderata:

· (A) Low Decision Latency. For some safety‑critical control loop, a decision must be taken within time $\ell_{\max} < 2\Delta$ after a state change is detected by any node.
· (B) Full‑Network State Consistency. At the moment a control command is issued, the controller’s view of the network state is the actual state of every node at some recent instant; the controller cannot act on inconsistent or guessed states that are not guaranteed to be fresh.
· (C) Robustness to a Single Link Failure. The network remains safe even if one communication link between any node and Earth fails permanently. The controller must still produce a safe command using whatever information arrives.

5.2 Impossibility Proof

Theorem 5.1 (Lunar CAP Impossibility).
No deterministic Earth‑resident centralized control policy can simultaneously satisfy (A), (B), and (C) for any ASN whose safe operation requires coordinating the states of at least two distinct surface nodes.

Proof. Assume such a policy $\mathcal{C}_{\text{Earth}}$ exists. Consider a minimal network with two surface nodes, $A$ (a depot) and $B$ (a transformation node), and Earth controller $E$. A safety constraint requires that whenever $A$’s buffer crosses a critical low threshold, $B$ must immediately ramp up production to prevent stockout; the total decision loop must close within $\ell_{\max} < 2\Delta$. To compute the correct command, the controller needs the true states of both $A$ and $B$.

At time $t_0$, $A$ sends a state update to Earth, taking time $\Delta$ to arrive. $B$ also sends its update. To satisfy (B)—full‑network state consistency—the controller must base its command on information that is guaranteed to reflect the actual state of both nodes at the moment of the critical event. Without waiting for $B$’s message, the controller has no guarantee of $B$’s state, violating (B). Thus the policy must wait for both messages.

Now consider two indistinguishable executions from the perspective of $E$:

· Execution 1: $B$’s state is normal, but the Earth‑$B$ link fails immediately after $B$ sends its update; the message never reaches Earth. Earth receives only $A$’s message.
· Execution 2: $B$’s state is abnormal (its production line has failed), and the Earth‑$B$ link fails at the same instant, again causing Earth to receive only $A$’s message.

Since the controller sees identical inputs (only $A$’s message) and must act within $\ell_{\max}$ (by (A)), a deterministic policy outputs the same command in both executions. Yet the safe command differs: in Execution 1, $B$ should be ordered to ramp up; in Execution 2, a different emergency action is required because $B$ cannot produce. Hence one of the two executions leads to an unsafe state, violating (C) (robustness to a single link failure). If instead the policy waits indefinitely for $B$’s message, it violates (A) because the latency exceeds $\ell_{\max}$. Any attempt to cache $B$’s old state would violate (B), as the cached view may be arbitrarily stale. ∎

Corollary 5.2 (Governance Mapping).
Any control loop with required response time $< 2\Delta$ must be executed by an autonomous (on‑Moon) controller. Only control loops with response tolerance $\ge 2\Delta$ may involve Earth in the decision loop. This splits authority along a physical, non‑negotiable boundary.

Proof. Immediate from Theorem 5.1: centralized Earth involvement violates the impossibility; therefore, the only way to meet all three requirements for fast loops is to move the controller to the lunar surface, eliminating the $2\Delta$ penalty. ∎

This result instantiates the classical distributed‑systems CAP theorem (Brewer, 2000; Gilbert & Lynch, 2002) in the domain of space supply networks, where the “partition” is the forced communication delay and link failures.

