Chapter 7. Open Problems and Future Work

The dissertation’s framework suggests several immediate extensions.

Open Problem 0 (Distributed‑Information Min‑Cut Gap).
Characterize the approximation ratio of a latency‑constrained, neighbour‑only distributed flow algorithm relative to the global min‑cut bound of Theorem 1. Under what conditions (e.g., network topology, buffer capacities, demand predictability) can a local‑information policy guarantee a fraction of the min‑cut throughput, and what is the worst‑case penalty of decentralization? This problem bridges network flow theory and real‑time distributed control and is the load‑bearing joint between Theorems 1 and 3.

Open Problem 1 (Resilience Trajectory Bounds).
Derive a closed‑form bound on the recovery time $\tau_{\text{recover}} = \inf\{t \ge t_0 : \rho_{\mathcal{N}}(t) \ge 1-\epsilon\}$ as a function of the network’s edge‑expansion properties and buffer capacities. This would generalise percolation‑theoretic robustness results to flow‑limited supply networks.

Open Problem 2 (Endogenous Pricing of ISRU Production).
Couple the min‑cut term of Theorem 1 with the mechanism‑design toolkit of Profession 19. When multiple colonies share ISRU output, a market must clear such that the shadow price of production capacity equals the marginal value of bypassing the min‑cut. This would unify Professions 19 and 20 under a common formal model.

Open Problem 3 (Three‑Body Extension).
Extend Theorem 3 to the Earth–Moon–Mars system where $\Delta$ is not constant but varies with orbital geometry. The delayed‑contract problem (Profession 21) can be formalized as a stochastic game with time‑varying information constraints, and the impossibility result would partition the control architecture into a hierarchy of autonomy domains that shift with planetary configuration.

Appendix A. Proofs for Chapter 3 (Theorem 1)

A.1 Network Model and Pure‑Transit Subnetwork

For a fixed resource $k \in \mathcal{R}$, let $\mathcal{N}_k = (V, E, C_k)$ be the directed graph with per‑resource capacity function $C_k : E \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$. We are given a set $S \subseteq V_S$ of source nodes that only inject the resource into the network (their net production equals outflow, and they have no internal consumption of $k$), and a distinguished sink node $v \in V_D$.

Definition A.1 (Pure‑Transit Node). A node $w \in V \setminus (S \cup \{v\})$ is pure‑transit for resource $k$ if, for any feasible flow $f$ satisfying Definition 2.2, the net flow of $k$ into $w$ equals the net flow out of $w$ (i.e., $w$ neither consumes nor permanently stores the resource; any local transformation either does not involve $k$ or is exactly balanced by an equal sink). Equivalently, in the conservation equation

\frac{dB_w}{dt} = \sum_{e \in \delta^-(w)} f(e,t) - \sum_{e \in \delta^+(w)} f(e,t) + P_w(t) - D(w,t),

we require $P_w(t) \equiv D(w,t)$ for all $t$, so that the buffer $B_w$ remains constant and the flow through $w$ is conserved.

Assumption A.1 (Pure‑Transit Condition). In this appendix, we assume that all nodes in $V \setminus (S \cup \{v\})$ are pure‑transit for resource $k$. This allows a direct application of the classical max‑flow min‑cut theorem.

A.2 Max‑Flow Min‑Cut Theorem (Recap)

Theorem A.1 (Ford & Fulkerson, 1956). Let $G = (V, E, c)$ be a directed graph with capacity function $c: E \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$, a source $s$, and a sink $t$. Then the maximum value of an $s$–$t$ flow is equal to the minimum capacity of an $s$–$t$ cut, where a cut is a partition $(X, \bar X)$ with $s \in X$, $t \in \bar X$, and its capacity is $\sum_{e \in \delta^+(X)} c(e)$.

A.3 Proof of Theorem 3.1 (Survivability Bound)

Proof. We construct an augmented graph $G^*$ by adding a supersource $s^*$ and directed arcs $(s^*, s)$ for every $s \in S$, each with infinite capacity (or capacity $+\infty$). The sink is $v$. By Assumption A.1, all intermediate nodes are pure‑transit, so flow is conserved at every node other than $s^*$ and $v$. Hence any flow that originates from the supersource and terminates at $v$ corresponds to a feasible flow in the original network, with the same net flow into $v$.

Consider any feasible flow policy that maintains $B_v(t) \ge 0$ for all $t$. Over a long time interval $[0,T]$, the integral of the buffer balance gives

B_v(T) - B_v(0) = \int_0^T \left( \sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} f(e,t) - \sum_{e \in \delta^+(v)} f(e,t) + P_v(t) - D(v,t) \right) dt.

Since $B_v(t) \ge 0$ and $B_v$ is bounded above by some maximum storage capacity $\bar B$, the left‑hand side is $O(1)$. Dividing by $T$ and taking $T \to \infty$, the average buffer change tends to zero, yielding

\lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T} \int_0^T D(v,t) \, dt = \lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T} \int_0^T \left( \sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} f(e,t) - \sum_{e \in \delta^+(v)} f(e,t) + P_v(t) \right) dt .

Let $\bar r(v)$ denote the time‑averaged sustainable consumption rate. Then

\bar r(v) \le \lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T} \int_0^T \sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} f(e,t) \, dt \;+\; \dot P_v(k),

since outgoing flows can only decrease net inflow and $P_v(t) \le \dot P_v(k)$.

Now observe that the time‑averaged inflow $\frac{1}{T}\int_0^T \sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} f(e,t)dt$ is exactly the average value of the flow entering $v$ from the rest of the network. Because the flow at each instant respects arc capacities and conservation at pure‑transit nodes, the flow vector $f(\cdot, t)$ is an $s^*$–$v$ flow in $G^*$ of value $\sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} f(e,t)$. By the max‑flow min‑cut theorem (Theorem A.1), for every $t$,

\sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} f(e,t) \le \text{max-flow}(G^*, s^*, v) = \operatorname{mincut}(G^*, s^*, v).

The min‑cut in $G^*$ cannot use any of the infinite‑capacity arcs from $s^*$ to $S$, so it must be a cut in the original graph separating $S$ from $v$; its capacity is exactly $\operatorname{mincut}_k(\mathcal N, S, v)$. Hence the time‑averaged inflow is also bounded by this constant, and

\bar r(v) \le \operatorname{mincut}_k(\mathcal N, S, v) + \dot P_v(k).

A sustainable steady‑state rate $r(v)$ is one for which there exists a policy maintaining $B_v \ge 0$ indefinitely; for any such policy the time‑average consumption equals $r(v)$, so the bound holds for all sustainable rates. $\square$

Tightness. If we have global knowledge of the network and can pre‑compute a maximum flow assignment, the bound is achievable: route a constant flow equal to the min‑cut capacity along the flow‑carrying paths, and supplement with local production up to $\dot P_v(k)$. Thus the inequality is sharp under the pure‑transit assumption.

